-- Thinking tools across the grade bands of the four synthetic spaces.
-- Generated by scripts/generate_thinking_tool_threads_migration.mjs; do not hand-edit.
--
-- Four OpenEvo thinking tools (Tinbergen's Questions, Causal Mapping, Payoff Matrices, Analogy Mapping) become one thread each in
-- every space, with a station per use. Each space has its own strategy that follows its aim: the three disciplinary spaces keep
-- the tools inside their narrow vocabulary, and the Interdisciplinary space uses them across domains. Threads have status
-- 'proposed'. One paragraph per space is added to the project introduction. Literature rows are added only where missing.
-- Everything is AI-drafted and unreviewed by a subject expert.
-- Rollback: DELETE FROM lpm_threads WHERE slug LIKE 'thinking-tool-%' (stations go by cascade).

SET search_path = public, extensions;

-- Bio-Core
INSERT INTO lpm_threads (id, project_id, branch_id, slug, title, thread_type, connecting_idea, narrative, teaching_prompt, evidence_note, gaps, status)
VALUES ('f437c951-55f8-5f42-b936-d17c9f580c61', '28997544-0d87-4b05-9ee2-7c474513c108', '489efe08-9c39-4601-8f31-fa40dccc8d5b', 'thinking-tool-tinbergen', 'Thinking tool: Tinbergen''s Questions', 'vertical_horizontal', 'Four questions about one trait of a population: how it works now (mechanism), how an individual gets it (development), where earlier versions came from (evolutionary history), and what it does for survival and offspring number (function). K-2 and 3-5 use two or three. From 6-8 an arrow from function back to history shows selection. At 9-12 the first three rows each get an inside and an outside factor. That split is the deck''s. Rahwan et al. do not ground it.', 'Tinbergen''s Questions in Bio-Core. Role in this space: primary. Leads K-2 and 3-5, the closest match to the storybook studies that taught selection to children aged 5 to 8. It returns at 9-12 to test adaptation claims against rival explanations. In every band it keeps the origin of a trait apart from its spread. Form: Four questions about one trait of a population: how it works now (mechanism), how an individual gets it (development), where earlier versions came from (evolutionary history), and what it does for survival and offspring number (function). K-2 and 3-5 use two or three. From 6-8 an arrow from function back to history shows selection. At 9-12 the first three rows each get an inside and an outside factor. That split is the deck''s. Rahwan et al. do not ground it. What it must not bring in: No cultural history, family history, or human goals and values as causes. No thoughts, feelings or intentions in the mechanism row. Function means the effect on survival and offspring number of carriers, with no purpose or need. Breeders appear only as a fixed outside rule on who may breed, with no account of their reasons.', 'Where did this trait come from, and what happened to the animals that had it? Which of those two questions did your answer address?', 'AI-drafted on 2026-10-26 from the OpenEvo Teacher''s Guide chapter on thinking tools, the MethodsBase records of the four tools and the verified evidence listed in the thread. No subject-matter expert has reviewed it and no classroom has tested it. Evidence used for the stations: Barton and Levstik (1996); Gelman and Wellman (1991); Kelemen (1999); Kelemen et al. (2014); Emmons et al. (2018); Brown et al. (2020); Shtulman et al. (2016); Kuhn (2007); Lira et al. (2024); Dewsbury (1992); Gregory (2009); Kelemen and Rosset (2009); Nehm and Reilly (2007); Laland et al. (2011); Tinbergen (1963). MethodsBase record: OE-METHOD-tinbergens-questions. Open checks for reviewers are listed under reviewNotes in scripts/data/thinking-tool-strategies.json.', '["No study found teaches the full grid to children under 11. The K-2 and 3-5 stations rest on storybook studies of selection (S13 to S15) and on studies of how children place events in time (S25).","The inside and outside split has no school study behind it. Rahwan et al. ground only the four classic questions, so the split appears at 9-12 only.","Need-based wording stayed common after teaching in several studies, so a station that works in one lesson may not hold.","Evidence for telling mechanism answers from function answers comes from university students working with scaffolds."]'::jsonb, 'proposed')
ON CONFLICT (project_id, slug) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('3aabc5dc-8dda-5893-829d-a2318c10ad09', 'f437c951-55f8-5f42-b936-d17c9f580c61', '8e62e70a-437d-540d-83f6-90a0fe54783a', 1, 'K-2, Noticing Variation and Family Resemblance (precursor). Children study picture cards of a dog mother, father and puppies. They answer one question with cards: where did this puppy''s coat colour come from? They place a parent card beside the puppy and say ''it got it from''. Then they answer a second question: what changes as this puppy grows? They add a grown-dog card at the end of the strip. Example: Coat colour in a litter of puppies and their parents, including a puppy that matches only one parent. Shown as: Picture-card strip: parents, puppy, grown dog, with two question cards. New demand: Keep two kinds of ''before'' apart: what came from the parents and what happens during growth. Evidence for sorting causes by type is thin at this age. Concepts: Inheritance, Population.', 'cae5dbad-0106-51c0-8818-aaacab89470e', 'Leads into the next use of the tool.')
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('514ffea2-4964-554c-87cf-d17b7c864892', 'f437c951-55f8-5f42-b936-d17c9f580c61', 'aac5e307-a98f-50c6-ba5d-5cc6bcd7ebdf', 2, 'K-2, Organisms and Their Habitats (precursor). A picture story shows a group of animals with thick or thin fur meeting a cold winter. Children place a survivor card beside the winter card for each kind. They say what happened to the animals with thick fur. The teacher offers the sentence frame ''the ones with thick fur were more likely to live'' in place of ''they grew it to keep warm''. Example: Thick or thin fur in a group of animals through a cold winter. Shown as: Picture story with survivor cards placed beside a winter card. New demand: Ask what happened to animals with and without the trait. Young children explain traits by purpose or need, so the sentence frame moves the answer to survival. Concepts: Adaptation, Population.', 'd68724fa-f299-5752-a49b-789f6416270d', 'Leads into the next use of the tool.')
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('714e8f81-a309-5ea6-b89e-69b344619743', 'f437c951-55f8-5f42-b936-d17c9f580c61', '70cf40f5-cb5d-508a-95dc-d18eaa465e94', 3, '3-5, Heritable Traits and Simple Population Description (precursor). Students sort picture cards into two bins: came from the parents, or happened during this animal''s life. Cards show eye colour, a scar, a learned trick and the stripes on a cat''s coat. For each card they ask: could a baby get this from its parents? Cards they cannot place go to a question pile, which the class tests against a family example. Example: Eye colour, a scar, a learned trick and coat stripes sorted by origin. Shown as: Two sorting bins plus a question pile, using picture cards. New demand: Place each answer on a time frame: one life or earlier generations. Dates carry little meaning before grade 3, so generation cards stand in for years. Concepts: Inheritance, Population.', 'cae5dbad-0106-51c0-8818-aaacab89470e', 'Leads into the next use of the tool.')
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('33ebe780-09fb-5f36-b024-ea0a30bd80e5', 'f437c951-55f8-5f42-b936-d17c9f580c61', 'ae85f5be-aead-5819-99df-8b3d431f79d1', 4, '3-5, Trait Advantage and Simple Selection Scenarios (precursor). Students read a short story about green and brown beetles on dark soil with hungry birds. They fill a three-row strip: how the beetles look now, where the colours came from, and what happened to each colour. They predict the mix in the next generation and mark any answer that says the beetles needed to change. Example: Green and brown beetles on dark soil with hungry birds. Shown as: Three-row answer strip and a story card set. New demand: Join a question about origin and a question about survival in one account that runs across generations. Need-based wording stays common after teaching, so each answer is checked for it. Concepts: Natural Selection, Adaptation, Inheritance.', 'f0abb875-7374-5581-b9fc-5c18f78006cc', 'Leads into the next use of the tool.')
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('c1797fa0-f502-54a1-9221-c2dc53b61787', 'f437c951-55f8-5f42-b936-d17c9f580c61', '4f1b4d5d-6232-5ef5-b44c-a850224b58a5', 5, '6-8, The Four-Component Natural Selection Mechanism (full). Students fill a four-row sheet for tameness in foxes bred over many generations: how it works in the body now (a teacher card on stress hormones), how an individual fox gets it, where earlier versions came from, and what difference it makes to leaving offspring under the breeders'' rule. An arrow from the last row back to the third shows the next generation. Example: Tameness in silver foxes bred at Novosibirsk from 1959, where only the tamest were allowed to breed. Shown as: Four-row sheet with one return arrow from function to history. New demand: Give each row the right kind of cause. Students often answer a how-it-works question with a what-it-does answer, and the sheet keeps the rows apart. Concepts: Domestication, Selection Pressure, Fitness, Inheritance.', '990f97f0-2597-510a-8772-0f2d0dc45a3e', 'Leads into the next use of the tool.')
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('092f73cb-16c3-5901-a917-dafa1423f070', 'f437c951-55f8-5f42-b936-d17c9f580c61', '8a1c0b8f-8e98-526f-bd7a-8cb2795daa85', 6, '6-8, Genes, Alleles, and the Origin of Variation (precursor). Students ask two questions about a resistant variant: where did it first come from, and why did it become common? They match the first to a random copying change in one gene that made a new allele, and the second to survival and offspring number. Then they sort claim cards, such as ''the bacteria changed because the drug was there'', by the question each answers. Example: Antibiotic resistance in a bacterial population. The variant usually exists before the drug is used, and some bacteria also pick up resistance genes from each other. Shown as: Two-column claim sort: origin of the variant, spread of the variant. New demand: Keep the origin of a variant apart from its spread. Claims that a variant arose because it was needed are common and mix the two. Concepts: Mutation, Allele, Gene, Selection Pressure.', 'eeb66b9f-2d89-5cae-bed1-eac6f60037b1', 'Leads into the next use of the tool.')
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('8ec8ec30-1164-56aa-9d4f-18e92b026e25', 'f437c951-55f8-5f42-b936-d17c9f580c61', 'b8bf5ec4-fe31-593a-8177-15b8109517b4', 7, '9-12, Evaluating Adaptationist Claims (extended). Students take the claim ''the red colour of blood is an adaptation''. They write mechanism, development and evolutionary history answers, each with an inside factor and an outside factor, and a function answer. They then test the claim against two rivals, a by-product of a selected trait and drift, and list what each row would need to show. Example: The red colour of vertebrate blood, which comes from the iron-containing protein that carries oxygen. Shown as: Four-row grid with inside and outside columns and a list of rival explanations. New demand: Split each row into inside and outside factors and test rivals with the grid. Drift needs variants that differ and blood colour does not vary. The split is the deck''s, with no classroom study. Concepts: Adaptation, Fitness, Drift.', 'd68724fa-f299-5752-a49b-789f6416270d', 'Leads into the next use of the tool.')
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('3b7644f0-82e3-520d-b3eb-d72a8d98f601', 'f437c951-55f8-5f42-b936-d17c9f580c61', '60df174a-b374-5cd1-9d06-9d76ecc4af9f', 8, '9-12, Evidence, Deep Time, and Patterns of Extinction (extended). Students apply the grid to the flipper bones of whales. They record how the flipper works now, how it forms in the embryo and which ancestors it traces to, with inside factors (genes, body plan) and outside factors (water, land). They then list independent lines of evidence for the shared ancestor and keep the function row apart from that evidence. Example: Flipper bones of whales compared with the arm bones of land mammals. Shown as: Grid with inside and outside columns, plus an evidence list for common ancestry. New demand: Use the evolutionary history row to defend a claim of shared ancestry with several independent kinds of evidence. Scaffolds help students keep near and distant causes apart. Concepts: Phylogeny, Common Ancestor, Adaptation.', '3bb15af5-a22b-512d-a3fb-708bc38f3d87', NULL)
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO lpm_threads (id, project_id, branch_id, slug, title, thread_type, connecting_idea, narrative, teaching_prompt, evidence_note, gaps, status)
VALUES ('325a0572-0b3b-5000-8e5f-815580cf6633', '28997544-0d87-4b05-9ee2-7c474513c108', '489efe08-9c39-4601-8f31-fa40dccc8d5b', 'thinking-tool-causal', 'Thinking tool: Causal Mapping', 'vertical_horizontal', 'A map of population-level factors such as the share of a trait variant, offspring number, food supply and predation, joined by arrows marked plus or minus. A one-generation delay is drawn as a mark on the arrow. A loop shows selection feeding back on a heritable trait. K-2 and 3-5 use chains and one simple loop. Students ask which single factor, changed a little, would shift the outcome most.', 'Causal Mapping in Bio-Core. Role in this space: primary. Leads 6-8 and 9-12. Selection, drift and divergence are population quantities joined by feedback, which a signed-arrow map shows directly. The map also tests the one-way thinking and the habit of looking for a leader behind a group pattern that middle school students show. Form: A map of population-level factors such as the share of a trait variant, offspring number, food supply and predation, joined by arrows marked plus or minus. A one-generation delay is drawn as a mark on the arrow. A loop shows selection feeding back on a heritable trait. K-2 and 3-5 use chains and one simple loop. Students ask which single factor, changed a little, would shift the outcome most. What it must not bring in: No organism as a node that chooses, wants or plans. No habit, reward or emotion loops. No social norms, institutions or goals as factors. No node that acts as a leader or controller of a group pattern. Arrows say increases or decreases and never say tries to.', 'What arrow would you add so that the result feeds back to its own cause, and how many generations does that take?', 'AI-drafted on 2026-10-26 from the OpenEvo Teacher''s Guide chapter on thinking tools, the MethodsBase records of the four tools and the verified evidence listed in the thread. No subject-matter expert has reviewed it and no classroom has tested it. Evidence used for the stations: Gopnik et al. (2004); Schulz and Gopnik (2004); Keil (2006); Danish (2014); Grotzer and Basca (2003); Ben-Zvi Assaraf and Orion (2010); Sweeney and Sterman (2007); Kuhn (2007); Dickes et al. (2016); Ben-Zvi Assaraf and Orion (2005); Hmelo-Silver and Pfeffer (2004); Chi et al. (2012); Resnick (1996); Sweeney and Sterman (2000); Wilensky and Reisman (2006); Hanisch and Eirdosh (2020); Rozenblit and Keil (2002). MethodsBase record: OE-METHOD-causal-mapping. Open checks for reviewers are listed under reviewNotes in scripts/data/thinking-tool-strategies.json.', '["Drawing arrows between factors and spotting a loop is untested at K-2. The K-2 station is a chain only.","Most evidence on maps with loops comes from middle school and adults. The sickle-cell and tail-feather maps at 9-12 have no school study behind them.","Stock and flow ideas and delays are hard at every age tested, so the two-loop limit at 9-12 is a design choice that needs a trial.","The project''s own causal mapping paper (S10) was cited as the tool''s origin and was not read in full here."]'::jsonb, 'proposed')
ON CONFLICT (project_id, slug) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('c44dd845-8f13-5d17-9ad8-40c48d312052', '325a0572-0b3b-5000-8e5f-815580cf6633', '76c205d2-33d4-5532-ba5a-cab715380b5e', 1, 'K-2, Many Different Kinds of Living Things (precursor). Children arrange picture cards in a chain joined by arrow strings and read it aloud with ''then'': a long dry time, no water, the plants die, no seeds, none of that kind are left. Then they ask what else would have had to be true for the chain to stop, such as plants growing in another valley. Example: A long dry time and a kind of plant that grew in only one valley. Shown as: Picture cards joined by arrow strings, read aloud with ''then''. New demand: Join the cards into a chain with arrows. Explanations are often incomplete in ways learners do not notice, so ask what else was needed. Evidence for arrows between factors is thin at this age. Concepts: Extinction, Species, Population.', 'edfd16ab-0aca-5aa8-b79b-d059383c9d04', 'Leads into the next use of the tool.')
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('660932cd-dcf2-5e47-8f88-23f95b77c578', '325a0572-0b3b-5000-8e5f-815580cf6633', '70cf40f5-cb5d-508a-95dc-d18eaa465e94', 2, '3-5, Heritable Traits and Simple Population Description (precursor). Students map the number of ducks on a pond from two cards, number of births and population size, and draw arrows both ways. They label each arrow ''more leads to more'' and read the loop aloud. They add a card for deaths and test what it does to the loop. Example: The number of ducks on a pond, with births and population size. Shown as: Cards with labelled arrows drawn both ways to form a loop. New demand: Close the loop so the effect also acts back on its cause. One-way chains are common, and teaching the cycle type directly helped third graders. Concepts: Population.', 'ae00fa14-acc2-5c38-8d0b-ff39d58f839e', 'Leads into the next use of the tool.')
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('2975a74c-3759-5765-a057-a4bb18113fb9', '325a0572-0b3b-5000-8e5f-815580cf6633', 'ae85f5be-aead-5819-99df-8b3d431f79d1', 3, '3-5, Trait Advantage and Simple Selection Scenarios (precursor). Students map two kinds of snowshoe hare, one that turns white in winter and one that stays brown, when snow comes late. Cards: snow cover, hares that show against the ground, hares caught by foxes, hares left, young hares with their parents'' fur colour. They link cards with arrows, add a second cause, the number of foxes, and predict the next generation. Example: Winter-white and winter-brown snowshoe hares when snow comes late. Shown as: Cards with arrows; two arrows can point into one card. New demand: Hold two causes at once and carry the result into the next generation. Fourth graders often leave out a known cause when several act together. Concepts: Natural Selection, Inheritance, Population.', 'f0abb875-7374-5581-b9fc-5c18f78006cc', 'Leads into the next use of the tool.')
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('53960f2f-0017-5ae6-a79a-dd1d6fecf07f', '325a0572-0b3b-5000-8e5f-815580cf6633', '4f1b4d5d-6232-5ef5-b44c-a850224b58a5', 4, '6-8, The Four-Component Natural Selection Mechanism (full). Students build a signed-arrow map of peppered moths on soot-darkened trees: number of dark and light moths, births of each, the birds'' catch of each, and how well each hides. They label births as a reinforcing loop and the catch as a balancing loop, mark a one-generation delay, and compare which colour''s loops win. Example: Light and dark peppered moths on soot-darkened tree bark. Shown as: Cards with plus or minus arrows, a delay mark and loop labels. New demand: Draw two kinds of loop with a delay of one generation. One-way thinking and missing delays are common at this age, so every arrow is checked for a way back. Concepts: Natural Selection, Fitness, Selection Pressure.', 'f0abb875-7374-5581-b9fc-5c18f78006cc', 'Leads into the next use of the tool.')
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('fbf317ae-a3ad-5a0e-b316-ded61c567f78', '325a0572-0b3b-5000-8e5f-815580cf6633', 'ca152c60-d9a5-509f-afdb-e2e7bdf983b4', 5, '6-8, How Populations Become New Species (full). Students map apple maggot flies, where one group prefers hawthorn fruit and another prefers apple fruit, and each mates on its fruit. Cards: fruit preference, matings between the groups, young of mixed pairs, season when adults appear. They look for a loop in which less mixing allows more difference, which allows less mixing, and mark a generation delay. Example: Apple maggot flies that prefer hawthorn fruit or apple fruit and mate on that fruit. Shown as: Cards with plus or minus arrows, one reinforcing loop and a generation delay mark. New demand: Add a second, independent cause and a loop that strengthens a split. Students look for a controlling agent behind group patterns, so check that no card works like a leader. Concepts: Speciation, Population, Selection Pressure, Species.', 'd79b98a7-92f7-5e80-be19-4223c8d86e01', 'Leads into the next use of the tool.')
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('87ea8f25-c2f0-5b21-9d3b-5030f1fe0f0f', '325a0572-0b3b-5000-8e5f-815580cf6633', '6c0b740a-cb12-5c3d-8a32-75fc01624ddf', 6, '9-12, Population-Level Genetics and Allele Frequency (extended). Students map the sickle-cell allele of the haemoglobin gene in a region with malaria. They link malaria risk, survival of each genotype, births of each genotype and the allele''s share in the next generation. They mark the loop that raises the share and the loop that lowers it, then check the map against a table of genotype survival. Example: The sickle-cell allele in a region where malaria occurs. Shown as: Signed-arrow map with one reinforcing and one balancing loop and a delay mark. New demand: Test the map against data: the loops predict where the share settles, and the survival table must agree. Revise any arrow that fails. Two loops only, since stocks and delays are hard for adults. Concepts: Allele, Gene, Selection Pressure, Fitness.', 'd34950cc-ba40-5ea7-9d9c-37a4dd862ac3', 'Leads into the next use of the tool.')
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('6f2b1ffd-4c4d-56bf-b489-69d46d76c358', '325a0572-0b3b-5000-8e5f-815580cf6633', 'b8bf5ec4-fe31-593a-8177-15b8109517b4', 7, '9-12, Evaluating Adaptationist Claims (extended). Students map long tail feathers in male birds with two selection pressures pointing opposite ways: tails raise mating success and lower survival. They add population size, which sets how much chance matters, and look for the tail length at which the two balance. They ask which single factor, changed a little, would shift the outcome most. Example: Long tail feathers in male birds that raise mating success and lower survival. Shown as: Signed-arrow map with two opposing arrows into one trait and a population-size card. New demand: Hold two opposing pressures and a chance factor in one map. People overrate how well they understand a mechanism, so students trace one arrow aloud. Concepts: Sexual Selection, Selection Pressure, Fitness, Drift.', '49575001-a64f-5de1-8962-3c89e297d7bb', NULL)
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO lpm_threads (id, project_id, branch_id, slug, title, thread_type, connecting_idea, narrative, teaching_prompt, evidence_note, gaps, status)
VALUES ('1eb3f799-6d01-5e6d-a709-b85130d447bd', '28997544-0d87-4b05-9ee2-7c474513c108', '489efe08-9c39-4601-8f31-fa40dccc8d5b', 'thinking-tool-payoff', 'Thinking tool: Payoff Matrices', 'vertical_horizontal', 'A table of outcomes for each pairing of two heritable types, where the outcome is fitness: expected number of surviving offspring. A type is a trait variant, and nothing is chosen. K-2 and 3-5 use an outcome table for one variant against one condition, filled with counters. From 6-8 the second party is another type or population, and each cell depends on both. Short term against long term means generations.', 'Payoff Matrices in Bio-Core. Role in this space: limited. The richest uses of the tool (choice, trust, fairness, institutions) lie outside Bio-Core. What remains is a table of fitness effects for heritable types. It appears as an outcome table at K-2 and 3-5 and as a two-type matrix from 6-8, mostly in one strand. Form: A table of outcomes for each pairing of two heritable types, where the outcome is fitness: expected number of surviving offspring. A type is a trait variant, and nothing is chosen. K-2 and 3-5 use an outcome table for one variant against one condition, filled with counters. From 6-8 the second party is another type or population, and each cell depends on both. Short term against long term means generations. What it must not bring in: No intentions, trust, fairness, communication, punishment or institutions as ways out of a dilemma. No strategy that is chosen or weighed. No money, scores or rewards as payoffs. A payoff is expected offspring, and a better outcome for all comes only from a changed environment or a different mix of types.', 'What would the table look like if the other group had mostly the other type? Which type leaves more offspring in each case?', 'AI-drafted on 2026-10-26 from the OpenEvo Teacher''s Guide chapter on thinking tools, the MethodsBase records of the four tools and the verified evidence listed in the thread. No subject-matter expert has reviewed it and no classroom has tested it. Evidence used for the stations: Kelemen et al. (2014); Emmons et al. (2018); Dickes and Sengupta (2013); Kuhn (2007); Shtulman et al. (2016); Flobbe et al. (2008); Wilensky and Reisman (2006); Resnick (1996); Sweeney and Sterman (2007); Steinberg et al. (2009). MethodsBase record: OE-METHOD-payoff-matrices. Open checks for reviewers are listed under reviewNotes in scripts/data/thinking-tool-strategies.json.', '["The payoff studies in the evidence base cover children''s choices in games. They do not carry over, because a Bio-Core payoff involves no choice. No study found tests reading a fitness table.","Evidence is thin for K-2 and 3-5. The counter games are a design proposal that needs a trial.","Bio-Core cannot use the dilemma-and-institutions side of the tool, which is the part the Teacher''s Guide stresses.","Four of five stations sit in strand B2, so the tool reaches few strands."]'::jsonb, 'proposed')
ON CONFLICT (project_id, slug) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('42a75ee2-0860-53f8-aebf-8ab56dfa3186', '1eb3f799-6d01-5e6d-a709-b85130d447bd', 'aac5e307-a98f-50c6-ba5d-5cc6bcd7ebdf', 1, 'K-2, Organisms and Their Habitats (precursor). Children play a card game. Ten green and ten brown caterpillar cards go on a green leaf mat, and a child playing the bird picks up the cards it spots first. They fill a two-box outcome table with the numbers left of each colour. They say which colour was more likely to live and what the next group might look like. Example: Green and brown caterpillars on a green leaf with a hungry bird. Shown as: Counter cards and a two-box table of survivors for each colour. New demand: Read one number from each box and compare them. Evidence that children this young can read an outcome table is thin. Concepts: Adaptation, Population.', 'd68724fa-f299-5752-a49b-789f6416270d', 'Leads into the next use of the tool.')
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('8b8a963e-87b3-5690-b64f-0c159bc2de3a', '1eb3f799-6d01-5e6d-a709-b85130d447bd', 'ae85f5be-aead-5819-99df-8b3d431f79d1', 2, '3-5, Trait Advantage and Simple Selection Scenarios (precursor). Students play a counter game with long-billed and short-billed birds and two flower types, deep and shallow. Nectar collected stands for chicks raised. They record it in a two-by-two table, one box for each bill and flower pairing, total each bill type over two seasons, and predict which bill will be more common after several generations. Example: Long and short bills of birds at deep and shallow flowers. Shown as: Two-by-two outcome table filled from a counter game. New demand: Add a second condition so the best type depends on the setting. Fourth graders can drop a known factor when several act together, so students read each row and column aloud. Concepts: Natural Selection, Adaptation, Inheritance.', 'f0abb875-7374-5581-b9fc-5c18f78006cc', 'Leads into the next use of the tool.')
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('55d0cefb-98a8-5c91-88f5-61fdf49e62cf', '1eb3f799-6d01-5e6d-a709-b85130d447bd', '4f1b4d5d-6232-5ef5-b44c-a850224b58a5', 3, '6-8, The Four-Component Natural Selection Mechanism (full). Students fill a two-by-two matrix for two heritable male types, fighters and display-only, meeting over mates. Each cell holds offspring expected for the row type against the column type, with a fight costing more than the prize. They work out each type''s average in a population mostly of one type, then mostly of the other, and say which spreads. Example: Two male types competing for mates: fighters and display-only males. Shown as: Two-by-two matrix of expected offspring, filled from a card game. New demand: Read a table where each outcome depends on the other type. Reasoning about another player''s mind is not needed here, which avoids a difficulty seen in children aged 8 to 10. Concepts: Fitness, Sexual Selection, Selection Pressure, Natural Selection.', '3f10e010-2780-57e2-aae0-62631276a8b0', 'Leads into the next use of the tool.')
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('78d2ae1e-2f5b-5834-8f06-5dc54fd5c166', '1eb3f799-6d01-5e6d-a709-b85130d447bd', 'ca152c60-d9a5-509f-afdb-e2e7bdf983b4', 4, '6-8, How Populations Become New Species (full). Students fill a matrix for matings between two frog populations whose calls have become different. Rows are the female''s population and columns the male''s, and each cell shows the fertile young expected. They see that mixed pairs leave fewer young and discuss how this keeps the two groups from blending over generations. Example: Matings within and between two frog populations whose calls differ. Shown as: Two-by-two matrix of fertile young for each pairing. New demand: Link the low success of mixed pairs to a split that widens over generations. Say whether mixed pairs fail because females refuse the calls or because the young are less fit. Concepts: Speciation, Species, Population, Selection Pressure.', 'd79b98a7-92f7-5e80-be19-4223c8d86e01', 'Leads into the next use of the tool.')
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('65e9c7c9-1773-5383-a6b4-f41d58743e71', '1eb3f799-6d01-5e6d-a709-b85130d447bd', 'b8bf5ec4-fe31-593a-8177-15b8109517b4', 5, '9-12, Evaluating Adaptationist Claims (extended). Students fill a matrix for two heritable tree types, tall-growing and short-growing, in a stand competing for light. Each cell shows expected seeds, set by light captured minus the cost of a tall trunk. Tall growth wins against either neighbour yet leaves both worse off than if both were short. They ask what change in the environment could alter the table. Example: Tall or short growth in neighbouring trees that compete for light. Shown as: Two-by-two matrix of expected seeds for each pairing of tree types. New demand: See that a type can spread while lowering the average for the population. Students may read short term against long term as planning, so say that no tree weighs anything. Concepts: Adaptation, Fitness, Selection Pressure, Natural Selection.', 'd68724fa-f299-5752-a49b-789f6416270d', NULL)
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO lpm_threads (id, project_id, branch_id, slug, title, thread_type, connecting_idea, narrative, teaching_prompt, evidence_note, gaps, status)
VALUES ('d51ce51c-dbcf-522d-b452-4b48439a6eb8', '28997544-0d87-4b05-9ee2-7c474513c108', '489efe08-9c39-4601-8f31-fa40dccc8d5b', 'thinking-tool-analogy', 'Thinking tool: Analogy Mapping', 'vertical_horizontal', 'Two or three biological cases side by side, shared structure in rows, and a column for where the comparison breaks. Rows ask what varies, what is inherited and what sets success. Students also compare a model (beads, cards, a simulation) with the case it stands for and name what the model leaves out. K-2 uses matching cards with a same or different mark. Analogies to culture, learning or machines are left out.', 'Analogy Mapping in Bio-Core. Role in this space: supporting. Leads no band. It carries transfer between cases inside biology, checks a model against the case it stands for, and builds the habit of naming where a comparison fails. Evidence for naming break points with children is thin. Form: Two or three biological cases side by side, shared structure in rows, and a column for where the comparison breaks. Rows ask what varies, what is inherited and what sets success. Students also compare a model (beads, cards, a simulation) with the case it stands for and name what the model leaves out. K-2 uses matching cards with a same or different mark. Analogies to culture, learning or machines are left out. What it must not bring in: No analogy in which an animal chooses or plans. No cultural evolution, learning or classroom examples as cases. No machine lineages as equal cases. No human institutions such as honeybee ''democracy''. Where a comparison needs a designer or a goal, the map names that as a break point.', 'What matches in these two cases? Name one row where the match breaks, and say what would have to be true for it to hold.', 'AI-drafted on 2026-10-26 from the OpenEvo Teacher''s Guide chapter on thinking tools, the MethodsBase records of the four tools and the verified evidence listed in the thread. No subject-matter expert has reviewed it and no classroom has tested it. Evidence used for the stations: Kotovsky and Gentner (1996); Richland et al. (2006); Gentner (1988); Gentner and Toupin (1986); Alfieri et al. (2013); Gick and Holyoak (1983); Spiro et al. (1989); Coll et al. (2005); Grosslight et al. (1991); Schwarz et al. (2009); Gentner (1983); Richland and Simms (2015). MethodsBase record: OE-METHOD-analogy-mapping. Open checks for reviewers are listed under reviewNotes in scripts/data/thinking-tool-strategies.json.', '["Naming where an analogy breaks is untested for children under 11, so the break column at K-2 and 3-5 is a design proposal.","Students treat models as copies of reality into grade 11, so the break column at 6-8 and 9-12 is the demanding step.","No study found compares cases of speciation or domestication with school students.","Several supporting studies are adult or small samples, so the 9-12 stations rest on weaker evidence than the K-2 ones."]'::jsonb, 'proposed')
ON CONFLICT (project_id, slug) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('2c8a115b-bc56-59a3-82a4-22a4c34f8970', 'd51ce51c-dbcf-522d-b452-4b48439a6eb8', '76c205d2-33d4-5532-ba5a-cab715380b5e', 1, 'K-2, Many Different Kinds of Living Things (precursor). Children place a picture of a mammoth and a picture of an elephant side by side. For each row card (legs, what it ate, where it lived, hair) they mark ''same'' or ''different''. A look-alike card, such as a hairy dog, tests whether they match by job or by looks. The class talks about what a ''different'' mark tells us. Example: A mammoth that no longer lives and an elephant that does. Shown as: Two picture columns with row cards and same or different marks. New demand: Compare parts by the job they do. Children favour look-alike features over matching roles, and naming where a comparison breaks is untested at this age. Concepts: Species, Extinction.', 'c0d6c5da-a3f4-5338-9a53-8fe66748a013', 'Leads into the next use of the tool.')
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('d74a7ce1-8794-5e20-b09b-1e9334d1c9cf', 'd51ce51c-dbcf-522d-b452-4b48439a6eb8', 'a4cd9b9e-d3a1-52bd-b76f-eb6173ab7259', 2, '3-5, Species, Relatedness, and Extinction (precursor). Students lay a three-generation tree of a dog family beside a simple tree of dog, fox and cat. In rows they match ''two relatives share an ancestor further back'', ''closer relatives share a more recent ancestor'' and the branching shape. A ''where it breaks'' column records that species branches are whole groups and span far longer times. Example: A dog family''s three-generation tree compared with a tree of dog, fox and cat. Shown as: Two trees drawn side by side with a three-column match table. New demand: Name a limit of the comparison. Asking children where an analogy breaks is untested, so the teacher models the first entry. Concepts: Common Ancestor, Species.', 'adbf8407-2108-56e2-9137-9a5c4baaacd7', 'Leads into the next use of the tool.')
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('462d184d-a0d0-5fcc-91d7-6cbbacde231d', 'd51ce51c-dbcf-522d-b452-4b48439a6eb8', '4f1b4d5d-6232-5ef5-b44c-a850224b58a5', 3, '6-8, The Four-Component Natural Selection Mechanism (full). Students compare two cases in a three-column map: wild cabbage bred into kale, broccoli and Brussels sprouts, and ground finches in a drought year. Rows hold what varies, what is inherited, what sets which individuals leave offspring, and what happened to the group. A last column names where the comparison breaks. Example: Wild cabbage bred into kale, broccoli and Brussels sprouts, compared with ground finches in a drought year. Shown as: Three-column analogy map with a final column for where it breaks. New demand: Find shared structure across two cases that look different. The break column names who or what sets the selection pressure: breeders or the environment. Concepts: Domestication, Selection Pressure, Natural Selection.', '990f97f0-2597-510a-8772-0f2d0dc45a3e', 'Leads into the next use of the tool.')
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('f3457e5e-a504-5431-8f33-28fd037e3192', 'd51ce51c-dbcf-522d-b452-4b48439a6eb8', 'ca152c60-d9a5-509f-afdb-e2e7bdf983b4', 4, '6-8, How Populations Become New Species (full). Students compare how populations became separate in two cases: anole lizards on Cuba and Jamaica and stickleback fish in two British Columbia lakes. In rows they record the starting group, what separated them, what pressure differed, what kept them apart, and a small tree for each case. A break column notes what islands and lakes do differently. Example: Anolis lizards on Cuba and Jamaica compared with benthic and limnetic stickleback pairs in two British Columbia lakes. Shown as: Two-case map with shared-structure rows and a column for where it breaks. New demand: Compare two cases and then say what each case adds. Learners shown one case alone transfer less than learners who compare two. Concepts: Speciation, Phylogeny, Population, Selection Pressure.', 'd79b98a7-92f7-5e80-be19-4223c8d86e01', 'Leads into the next use of the tool.')
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('7d303d3e-fda3-533e-8519-10cc1751ec6f', 'd51ce51c-dbcf-522d-b452-4b48439a6eb8', '6c0b740a-cb12-5c3d-8a32-75fc01624ddf', 5, '9-12, Population-Level Genetics and Allele Frequency (extended). Students map a jar of coloured beads standing for two alleles onto an island population. Rows pair each step of the bead run (draw, refill, repeat) with a step in the population. A break column lists what the jar leaves out, such as mutation and the environment, and they predict how a small jar and a large jar differ. Example: A bead jar standing for the two alleles of a gene in an island population. Shown as: Two-column map with a third column for what the model leaves out. New demand: Judge a model by what it leaves out. Many students in grade 11 treat a model as a copy of reality. Concepts: Allele, Population, Drift, Mutation.', 'd34950cc-ba40-5ea7-9d9c-37a4dd862ac3', 'Leads into the next use of the tool.')
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lpm_thread_stations (id, thread_id, data_object_id, sequence, role_note, via_element_id, relation_to_next)
VALUES ('9ab464f2-9cd3-55dc-a1aa-a8d629972d34', 'd51ce51c-dbcf-522d-b452-4b48439a6eb8', '60df174a-b374-5cd1-9d06-9d76ecc4af9f', 6, '9-12, Evidence, Deep Time, and Patterns of Extinction (extended). Students compare the wings of birds, bats and insects in three columns. Rows ask what the wing does, which bones or structures it has, and which ancestor it traces to. They mark where shared function matches and where shared ancestry matches. A break column asks what each match can and cannot show about common ancestry. Example: Wings of birds, bats and insects compared by function and by ancestry. Shown as: Three-case map with function and ancestry rows and a break column. New demand: Separate similarity in what a structure does from similarity in descent. Comparing three cases guards against a single analogy locking in a wrong picture. Concepts: Phylogeny, Common Ancestor, Adaptation.', '3bb15af5-a22b-512d-a3fb-708bc38f3d87', NULL)
ON CONFLICT (thread_id, sequence) DO NOTHING;

UPDATE projects SET introduction = introduction || '

Thinking tools. Four OpenEvo thinking tools (Tinbergen''s Questions, Causal Mapping, Payoff Matrices and Analogy Mapping) are deployed across the grade bands of this space as four threads named "Thinking tool: ...". Bio-Core explains evolution as variation, inheritance and selection acting on populations, with no organism that wants, plans or steers. Tinbergen''s Questions keeps the question of where a trait came from apart from the question of what happened to the animals that had it. Causal Mapping draws selection as population quantities joined in loops. Analogy Mapping compares biological cases and checks a model against its case. Payoff Matrices read fitness effects of heritable types. The boundary removes goals, choice, culture and machines from all four. Tinbergen''s Questions leads in K-2 and 3-5. Picture stories separate the origin of a trait from what happened to its carriers, which prepares the B2 idea that traits spread without need. Causal Mapping leads in 6-8 and 9-12 and turns those answers into signed arrows, one-generation delays and loops. Payoff Matrices start as outcome tables in K-2 and 3-5 and become a two-type matrix in 6-8. Analogy Mapping supports transfer and model checking. The threads have the status proposed. AI drafted them and no subject expert has reviewed them.' WHERE id = '28997544-0d87-4b05-9ee2-7c474513c108' AND introduction NOT LIKE '%Thinking tools. Four OpenEvo thinking tools%';

